body:
Minecraft is a world of cubes, but light is continuous. That mismatch is where my fascination with shaders started. Earlier this year I opened two repositories: Euphoria-Rewrite, a rewrite of Complementary Shaders, and another one simply named GLSL, an enhanced pack based on BSL from the 1.7.10 era. They are the outlet for Methode's artisan instinct: a real toolchain should have its light tuned by hand.

Light can be handwritten

Complementary is one of the best-known shaderpacks in the community. The motive for rewriting it is not grand: I want a few particular beams of light to follow my own physical intuition, instead of waiting for the upstream's next version. The GLSL pack, meanwhile, has borrowed plenty of good work from others: Complementary's fog and noise for the Nether, Solas's black hole and accretion disk for the End, photon-style water waves and reflections, aurora ported from Lux, and the Twilight Forest dimension running Lux water configuration with aurora enabled. The plan list still holds Complementary's volumetric light mainline, underwater caustics, and End Beam enhancements.

Volumetric light is my favorite part to write, because the signature scene of this twilight citadel, those shafts slanting through the halls, is essentially an integral over scattering: light is scattered inside the medium, and every segment of the sightline contributes a little brightness. The principle takes one sentence; the difficulty is every small trade: how many steps to sample, how to jitter the noise, how to reuse the shadow map, each one haggling inside the frame budget. Sketched:

// volumetric light: dithered ray march along the view ray (simplified sketch)
float dither  = bayer64(gl_FragCoord.xy);
vec3  scatter = vec3(0.0);
for (int i = 0; i < STEPS; ++i) {
    float t = t0 + (float(i) + dither) * stepLen;
    vec3  p = eye + dir * t;
    scatter += shadow(p) * density(p) * stepLen;
}
color = color * transmittance + lightColor * scatter;

Tuning volumetrics teaches respect for baselines: one choice of jitter offset can lift a light shaft from cheap to sublime, and the shape of a density function decides whether dusk feels tender or desolate. Commits to these repos mostly land late at night. Research occupies the day with papers and code; shaders are the handicraft time I keep for myself: no benchmarks, no reviews, only pixels and frame rates.

> Light never explains itself; it simply lands where it should. Whoever writes light must explain every step on its behalf.

Why a researcher writes shaders

My day job is long-horizon agents; my hobby is chasing light inside a world of cubes. The two resemble each other more than they look: both start by building a model of a world, then keep asking it why it glows and why it darkens.

- Shaders are the most honest physics class: every lumen needs a provenance, and the compiler has no patience for eloquence.
- Porting and homage are community tradition; reading someone's fog, aurora, or black hole is also reading their aesthetic.
- Optics gives research a gentle metaphor: only the small fraction scattered into your eye is ever visible; the rest keeps traveling through the medium.

Late at night a shaft of light finally slants in through the window and lands on the blocky ground. The satisfaction feels no different from the moment an evidence chain closes. Light lands where it should, and that is enough.
